Revision note: This document is the post-feedback version of the Technical Plan. Changes
introduced in response to the feedback are highlighted in blue throughout the report. The original
pre-feedback submission is Version 1.0.

1 Introduction and project scope

SafeVoice is a Streamlit application designed to collect information about human trafficking-related
incidents. Its current processing workflow captures a report through a web form, stores a raw JSON
representation, flattens the payload into a Pandas DataFrame, applies cleaning and coordinate
rounding, generates RDF N-Triples, and uploads the triples to AllegroGraph [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].

    The objective of this technical plan is to assess whether the current application supports reliable
data transformation, semantic interoperability and responsible handling of sensitive information in
the context of the HDS. The report is organised around the four course windows. It first documents
the existing architecture and data model, then inventories and analyses the issues, and finally
proposes improvements linked to the evidence.

    The project distinguishes between:
• Verified code findings: behaviour directly visible in the inspected repository.

• Requirements and design gaps: capabilities requested by experts or needed for the intended
    HDS role but not demonstrated in the inspected code.

• External dependencies: capabilities that depend on infrastructure or decisions outside the

                          3
Data Science in Practice                                                    SafeVoice Technical Plan

    current project, such as IPA organisational SSO, protected document storage and a live FDP
    endpoint.
    The implementation proposal is intentionally realistic. The prototype is expected to run locally
and continue using AllegroGraph. Local authentication and application-level access checks can be
developed as prototype functionality. Organisational identity management, production document
storage and full FDP registration are treated as future integration requirements unless the relevant
infrastructure becomes available.

2 Existing SafeVoice architecture

2.1 Application and data workflow

The repository separates the application interface, orchestration, cleaning, RDF mapping and
AllegroGraph ingestion. The Streamlit interface builds a nested JSON payload. The payload is
flattened with pandas.json_normalize and passed to the orchestrator. The orchestrator calls
the cleaning module, generates an N-Triples file through the mapper, and passes the result to the
ingestion module. After successful ingestion, the original JSON and generated N-Triples file are
moved to processed directories [2, 4, 3, 5, 6].

