Data Science in Practice     SafeVoice Technical Plan

2.2 CDM hierarchy

The current CDM CSV organises fields into six levels: Record, Incident, Victim, Trafficker, Publica-
tion and Updates [8]. These levels provide a logical grouping of information rather than a complete
relational database specification. The hierarchy should be interpreted together with the actual field
names, requiredness and the RDF relationships defined in the mapping.

• Record: source and record identifiers, reporting language and dates.

• Incident: incident identifier, departure and destination information, and incident description.

• Victim: victim identifier, number of victims, nationality, age, gender, situation and current
    location.

• Trafficker: identifier and available descriptive information.

• Publication: publication name, link and date.

• Updates: update identifier, update type, field path and criticality indicator.

    The presence of an Updates level in the CDM does not by itself establish that append-only
correction handling is implemented. The inspected form and pipeline need an explicit record-
versioning and relationship model to support that behaviour.

2.3 How the current RDF mapping works

The YAML mapping defines prefixes such as ex, cdm, schema, prov and xsd. It defines resources
for a case, victim, trafficker, current victim location, departure location, destination location and
publication. For each DataFrame row, the mapper substitutes field placeholders into subject and
object templates, expands prefixes and writes RDF triples in N-Triples format [5, 7].

    For example, the case resource is linked to victim and trafficker resources using cdm:hasVictim
and cdm:hasTrafficker. The mapping also links the case to departure and destination resources.
This is the intended semantic structure. However, the mapper handles missing values unevenly.
When the placeholder of a literal value does not match a DataFrame column, the corresponding
triple is silently skipped. When a placeholder inside an IRI template is empty, the triple is not
skipped and the mapper produces a truncated IRI such as ex:victim_. In both cases, a syntactically
valid N-Triples file does not guarantee that all intended CDM fields have been represented correctly.

3 Issue inventory and analysis across the four HDS windows

The inventory below focuses on issues that can be supported by the inspected code or are clearly
identified as missing capabilities in the inspected processing path. A missing stage is not presented
as proof that no external or infrastructure-level control exists. Findings W1-01, W1-03, W1-05,
W1-06 and W3-04 can be reproduced by running the repository code, as described in section A.2.

    The findings are prioritised using the MoSCoW method:
• Must: Essential for a reliable and safe prototype.

• Should: Important improvements that should be implemented if time permits.

• Could: Useful improvements that can be included if resources allow.

• Won’t: Outside the current implementation scope and reserved for future work.

3.1 Window 1: ETL safety and pipeline integrity

Window 1 concerns the reliability of capture, validation, cleaning and transformation. The following
findings are directly supported by the current code.

