    The five design decisions described in the same work are also relevant: input structuring,
controlled vocabularies, domain ontology alignment, data-space ontology structuring and RDF
specification/serialisation [12]. In SafeVoice, these decisions translate into a consistent form/CDM
contract, stable vocabulary identifiers, agreed predicates and relationships, an explicit topic-record-
document model, and validated RDF output.

    The VODAN-Africa case demonstrates the use of semantic technologies, controlled vocabularies
and RDF to support data interoperability in a federated setting [13]. It provides a relevant precedent
for the direction of SafeVoice, but it does not mean that SafeVoice is already registered or operational
within an FDP.

5 Proposed improvements and target architecture

The proposed improvements directly address the issues identified in the four windows. The priority
is to establish a reliable data contract, improve the reporting workflow and enforce access control
before expanding the system’s production capabilities.

5.1 Topic-based, narrative-first reporting

The expert discussion with Mirjam van Reisen identified a need to organise work around topics
rather than treating every report as an isolated form submission. A topic should group related
records. A user should be able to create a topic or select an existing one, enter a narrative first, and
then provide optional structured information where available [10].

                          16
Data Science in Practice      SafeVoice Technical Plan

    Each submitted record should receive a unique identifier and a server-generated timestamp.
Corrections should be stored as new records linked to the earlier record, preserving the submission
history rather than overwriting it. The final fields and exact correction relationships should be
confirmed with Mirjam and Daniel before implementation.

5.2 Proposed topic-record-document model

The proposed conceptual model is:
• Topic: a container for a subject or case area, with a stable topic identifier.

• Record: an immutable, timestamped submission linked to one topic.

• Correction: a new record linked to the record it corrects or supersedes.

• Document: a separately identified attachment linked to a record, with metadata such as title,
    file type, upload time and storage reference.

• User: an authenticated identity with permissions for the relevant topics, records and documents.

5.3 Document storage proposal

The current repository does not demonstrate an IPA document-storage service, so the plan does
not assume that one already exists. For the local prototype, the team may demonstrate document
metadata and, if time permits, attachment handling with dummy files. Real sensitive documents
should not be used in local demonstrations. For production, binary documents should be stored
in an approved protected server-side storage service. AllegroGraph should contain the document
identifier, metadata and authorised reference, not the binary content itself.

5.4 Access-control proposal

The prototype should replace the manually entered Source ID as the basis for access decisions with
local authentication. The application should derive the current user from the authenticated session
and enforce ownership checks whenever topics, records or documents are listed, opened or changed.
File and folder names should be generated on the server from the authenticated user and record
identifiers, never from typed text (W3-04). The authenticated identity should also be written to
the RDF graph as the responsible agent (W2-03). IPA organisational SSO is outside the current
implementation scope. The prototype should be designed so that its authentication layer can later
be replaced or integrated with an organisational identity provider.

5.5 FDP and HDS integration boundary

SafeVoice is the data-producing application. AllegroGraph is the current RDF persistence layer. An
FDP, where available, provides a metadata and discovery interface for datasets. The HDS is the
wider federated environment in which participating data sources and services can exchange and
discover data under agreed governance and interoperability conditions.

    The inspected repository demonstrates local data capture, RDF generation and AllegroGraph
ingestion. It does not demonstrate FDP registration. Therefore, the realistic project objective is
to improve the quality and consistency of the RDF and prepare dataset metadata for future FDP
integration. Actual registration requires an FDP endpoint, its metadata profile and the relevant
access arrangements.

